course: biology
lesson: 196
title: Working dogs: guide, sniffer, sheep, rescue and helper dogs
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Many dogs have real jobs. They use the things dogs are best at, a superb nose, sharp ears, speed, strength and a deep wish to work with people, to help us every day.
explain: Picture a blind woman walking to the shops: her guide dog stops dead at the kerb so she knows a road is there, and if she says «forward» while a car is coming, the dog refuses.
explain: At the airport a beagle sniffs a line of suitcases and suddenly sits down: it has smelled something it was trained to find, and the sit is its way of telling its handler.
explain: Working dogs are trained for months or years, and a dog wearing a jacket that says «do not distract» is busy; stroking or calling it could put its owner in danger.
fact: A guide dog stops at kerbs and steps so its blind owner knows they are there, and it learns intelligent disobedience: refusing an order that would lead its owner into danger.
fact: Sniffer dogs are trained to find particular smells such as drugs, food or explosives, and beagles are often used at airports because they are friendly and have excellent noses.
fact: A border collie herds sheep by running around them and bringing them to the shepherd, who guides the dog with whistles that carry far across a hillside.
fact: Rescue dogs can smell a person buried under snow or rubble and can search an area much faster than a line of people.
fact: Medical alert dogs can notice changes in a person's body, often by smell, and warn them, for example before a dangerous drop in blood sugar; scientists are also testing whether dogs can sniff out some illnesses.
fact: Husky teams pull sleds across snow, and sled dog races in Alaska run for more than a thousand kilometres.
fact: Therapy dogs visit hospitals, care homes and schools to help people feel calmer, and some schools have reading dogs that children read aloud to.
fact: Police dogs are trained to follow a scent trail and to find and hold people; when they retire they often live with their handler.
fact: Livestock guardian dogs grow up with sheep and treat them as their family, barking at and chasing away foxes and other predators, often at night.
fact: Hearing dogs help deaf people by touching them and leading them to sounds such as a doorbell, an alarm clock or a smoke alarm, and many were once rescue dogs given a new job.
fact: An assistance dog in a jacket marked «do not distract» is working, so you should not stroke, call or feed it without asking its owner.
exercise: Why does a guide dog stop at the edge of the pavement? | So its blind owner knows a kerb is there | Because it is tired and wants a short rest | Because it wants to sniff the road and the smells on it | Because it is scared of cars
exercise: What is intelligent disobedience? | Refusing an order that would put the owner in danger | Ignoring every single order just for fun and to show who is boss | Learning tricks without a trainer | Barking at strangers
exercise: A sniffer dog at the airport sits next to a suitcase. What does it mean? | It smelled something it was trained to find | It is tired of walking | It wants to go on holiday with whoever owns the case | It found a cat hiding inside the case
exercise: Why are beagles often used at airports? | They are friendly and have excellent noses | They are the biggest dogs, so people notice them | They can read labels on the bags and parcels | They never need walks
exercise: What is a border collie doing when it runs around a flock of sheep? | Herding them to the shepherd | Hunting them for food | Playing tag with them | Counting them
exercise: How does a shepherd give orders to a sheepdog far away on a hill? | With whistles | With a torch | With a mobile phone | With a drum
exercise: How does a rescue dog find a person under the snow after an avalanche, when snow crashes down a mountain? | By smelling the person through the snow | By hearing their heartbeat from a mile away | By seeing through the snow | By digging everywhere at random
exercise: Why are rescue dogs so useful? | They search much faster than a line of people | They can lift people out of the rubble all on their own | They can dig with machines | They never get cold, so they can work for days without stopping
exercise: A medical alert dog nudges a girl before she feels unwell. How might it know? | It can notice changes in her body, often by smell | It reads her mind, because dogs can hear people's thoughts | It looks at her phone | It is guessing
exercise: What do husky dogs do across snow? | Pull sleds | Dig tunnels | Herd reindeer | Carry people on their backs
exercise: Why does a therapy dog visit a hospital ward? | To help patients feel calmer and happier | To check patients' temperatures with its cold wet nose | To guard the doctors | To clean the floors
exercise: What do police dogs do? | Follow scent trails and find people | Write police reports | Direct traffic | Drive police cars
exercise: Why does a farm dog bark at a fox at night? | It guards the animals it thinks of as its family | It wants to play with the fox and is asking it to come closer | It is scared of the dark | It is calling other foxes
exercise: A hearing dog touches its owner when the doorbell rings. Who does it help? | A deaf person | A blind person | A person who cannot walk | A person who is asleep
exercise: A dog in a jacket marked do not distract is walking in a shop. What should you do? | Leave it alone, it is working | Call it over to stroke it | Give it a treat | Whistle to make it look
exercise: Where do many hearing dogs come from? | They were rescue dogs given a new job | They are all tame wolves | They are born deaf themselves, so they know how it feels | They come from the circus
test: «the guide dog won't go even tho the man said forward, bad dog innit» What is right? | No, it saw danger and refused on purpose, which is good | Yes, it is badly trained and should be sent back | Yes, it is lazy | No, guide dogs never move until the man pulls them along hard
test: «aww a doggy in a vest, lemme pet him» but the jacket says do not distract. What should you do? | Leave him alone so he can keep his owner safe | Pet him quickly | Call his name | Give him some crisps
test: In a cartoon a police dog drives the police car and reads people their rights. What do real police dogs do? | Follow scent trails and find people for their handler | Drive police cars | Give out parking tickets to people who park badly in the street | Arrest people alone and lock them in the cells
test: «why'd the airport dog sit by MY bag, i'm not a criminal» What is the likely answer? | It smelled something it was trained to find, maybe fruit or a sandwich | It liked the colour of the bag | It was tired | It always sits by random bags, just to keep the people at the airport busy
test: What would happen if a border collie had no shepherd's whistles to follow on a big hill? | It would find it much harder to know where to bring the sheep | It would eat the sheep | It would herd cows instead | It would still know exactly where to go, because collies read the shepherd's mind
test: «snow came down the mountain and a man's under it» Which helper could find him quickest? | A rescue dog sniffing over the snow | A line of people looking with their eyes | A cat | A sheepdog with whistles
test: Count them: a sled has 6 huskies pulling it. How many legs are pulling? | 24 | 12 | 6 | 18
test: Count them: 8 huskies and one driver at the finish line. How many ears are there, dogs and driver together? | 18 | 16 | 9 | 36
test: «guard dog's barking all night at the sheep field, shut it up!» What is it probably doing? | Warning off a fox to protect the sheep | Barking at nothing for fun | Asking for its dinner | Calling other dogs to a party
test: «why's there a dog in the school library lol» What is a likely reason? | It is a reading dog that children read aloud to | It is a police dog looking for stolen books on the shelves | It is lost | It is a sniffer dog for chocolate and sweets in the lunch boxes
test: «my deaf uncle's dog keeps pawing him every time the alarm goes» What is the dog doing? | It is a hearing dog telling him about the sound | It is hungry | It wants to play a game | It is scared of the alarm and wants your uncle to pick it up and hold it
test: Which job matches a dog that refuses to cross the road when a car is coming? | Guide dog | Sniffer dog | Sled dog | Herding dog
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct described as intelligent disobedience in the guide dog? | The refusal of a command whose execution would endanger the handler | The habitual disregard of all commands | The performance of tricks without instruction | The barking at unfamiliar persons
exam: Identify the principal reason for which beagles are frequently deployed at airports. | Their amiable temperament combined with an exceptionally acute sense of smell | Their unusually great size | Their capacity to read luggage labels and to understand the names of countries | Their freedom from any requirement for exercise
exam: It is given that a dog wearing a jacket bearing the words «do not distract» is encountered in a shop. Ascertain the appropriate conduct. | The animal is to be left undisturbed, since it is working | The animal is to be summoned and stroked | The animal is to be offered food | The animal is to be photographed at close range
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the location of a person buried beneath snow. | A trained rescue dog may detect the person by scent more rapidly than a line of searchers | Only a line of searchers using sight alone can locate such a person beneath the snow | Dogs are unable to detect scent through snow | Herding dogs are exclusively employed for this purpose
